{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And send you your other brother, and Benjamin.</b> The reason he did not mention Simeon by name appears to be that he was not pleasing to his father on account of the incident of Shechem, and therefore he did not say 'and send you Simeon.' And in the Midrash: 'and send you your brother' — this is Joseph; 'another' — this is Simeon. His intention at the time of prayer was to pray in general also about the other brother, perhaps he is still alive." "<b>And El Shaddai give you mercies before the man.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: a person should always precede prayer before trouble, for Jacob, before sending Benjamin, preceded with prayer." "<b>And in the Midrash.</b> 'And El Shaddai give you mercies' — that he saw fit to bless them with 'El Shaddai' teaches you that many sufferings passed over Jacob: while he was still in his mother's womb Esau contended with him, as it is said (Genesis 25:22) ״ויתרוצצו הבנים״. He fled from Esau to Laban and suffered, as it is said (ibid. 31) ״הייתי ביום אכלני חרב וקרח בלילה״ and so on. Once he escaped from Laban, Esau came to kill him; then the affliction of Dinah came upon him, and the affliction of Rachel. He sought to rest a little — and the anguish of Joseph came upon him. This is what is written (Job 3:26) ״לא שלותי ולא שקטתי ולא נחתי ויבא רוגז״. And he would not accept consolation, as it is said, ״וימאן להתנחם״; immediately the affliction of Simeon came upon him, and afterward the affliction of Benjamin. Therefore he prayed with 'El Shaddai' — He who said to the world 'enough' (די), may He say to my afflictions 'enough' (די). And so our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted (Psalms 20:2): ״יענך ה' ביום צרה ישגבך שם אלהי יעקב״ and so on — 'the Elohim of Jacob,' and it does not say 'the Elohim of Abraham and Isaac'; from here, for the owner of the beam, that he should enter the thick part of the beam. Rabbi Joshua ben Levi interpreted the verse concerning the exile: 'And El Shaddai give you mercies,' as it is written (ibid. 106) ״ויתן אותם לרחמים לפני כל שוביהם״. 'Before the man' — this is the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is written (Exodus 15:3) ״ה' איש מלחמה״. 'And send you your brother' — these are the ten tribes. 'Another, and Benjamin' — this is the tribe of Judah and Benjamin. 'And I, as I have been bereaved, have been bereaved' — I was bereaved in the destruction of the First Temple, I was bereaved in the destruction of the Second — 'I shall be bereaved no more' (Beresheet Rabbah). What Rabbi Joshua ben Levi interpreted — 'the man,' this is the Holy One, blessed be He — it is possible that he intended the attribute of mercy. And he said, 'And El Shaddai give you mercies' — this is the Holy One, blessed be He; that is, may His name, which is the attribute of mercy, be given to you. And he comes to explain that the prophet directed his intention in his prayer also for future generations, in the ingathering of exiles, that the Shekhinah dwells with us in exile — may it be exalted and unified with the attribute of mercy. And it is also possible that he intended the word 'the man' because it is impossible to understand this as referring to Joseph, since he already said ״וקומו שובו אל האיש״, and it would have sufficed to say 'may He give you mercies before him' — and what reason would there be to repeat 'the man' a second time? And because the words of the prophet are founded upon a precise line, even in his prayer, therefore it was necessary to interpret 'before the man' — this is the Holy One, blessed be He, who is El Shaddai, and it is as if he said: 'may He give you mercies before Him.' And the reason he did not say 'before Him' is to teach us that here he called El Shaddai 'the man,' and so we find (Exodus 15:3) ״ה' איש מלחמה״. And in the Midrash on Song of Songs (Song of Songs 1:1): ״אשכול הכופר״ — Ben Nezira said: 'cluster' (אשכול) — this is the Holy One, blessed be He; 'the man' (איש) in whom everything is."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 43, :notes {2 "The phrase 'לבעל הקורה שיכנס בעביה של קורה' is a Talmudic idiom (Berakhot 63a) meaning one who is beset by suffering should pray to the God associated with that suffering; rendered hyper-literally as 'for the owner of the beam, that he should enter the thick part of the beam.' The citation '(שיר השירים א׳:א׳)' attached to 'ובמדרש שיר השירים' appears to reference the Midrash collection rather than the biblical verse, since אשכול הכופר appears in Song of Songs 1:14, not 1:1."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:20:12.692430369Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}